  "subtitle": "The speech synthesizer you programmed by writing to the screen",
  "why_scout_cares": "A speech synthesis peripheral with a genuinely bizarre interaction model: you made it talk by writing ASCII-encoded phoneme codes to video memory. The synthesizer read the right half of the bottom screen line and spoke whatever phoneme string it found there. You didn't send it text — you wrote cryptic character sequences like '?K6MPY(UT?' directly onto the display, and the machine would read them back as speech. The mapping of phonemes to arbitrary ASCII characters (6=UH1, $=TH, +=NG) was like a secret language. This was phoneme-to-speech via screen memory — there was no text-to-speech algorithm at all. Built around the Votrax SC-01-A chip inside a potted epoxy module. $399 in 1979, discontinued 1983.",
  "search_terms": ["TRS-80 Voice Synthesizer", "26-1180", "Votrax SC-01", "Radio Shack speech synthesizer 1979"],
